and he tried to chop my finger  it broke my hand  this is virginia it's not her real name  she's scared her ex-husband is trying to  find and kill her that's why she's  seeking asylum here in the uk  he battered me on with a mallet  she says her husband joined a gang and  started abusing her torturing her  forcing her to be cut that is female  genital mutilation or fgm i didn't want  to undergo the cat so he started  he started beating me up torturing me  cutting me with my knife they forced me  to go  to  to undergo the cat but before then  they tortured me  afterwards she escaped and fled to  england  they are coming from my head and that is  when i decided to go because i knew they  were not joking  virginia has twice had her asylum claim  denied she's now appealed again  to claim asylum you need to show you are  persecuted and you can't get help in  your home country  there seems to be a sort of myth that  asylum is easy to get in this country  it's not it's really hard to get you  really have to prove to prove that a  state is unwilling or unable to help you  it's a very high threshold  the home office says it takes the  well-being of asylum seekers incredibly  seriously and considers sensitively all  the evidence in their claims including  of domestic violence or female genital  mutilation  and it says it's working with countries  around the world to try and prevent  crimes like fgm happening in the first  place  but campaign has worn the rigidity of  the asylum system means women and girls  could still fall through the cracks it's  really important to recognize that  um  our policies don't match our rhetoric in  terms of protecting women and girls from  violence around the world the grounds  for protection under the refugee  convention are very narrow and very  strict and definitely too narrow  behind all the legal complexity this is  a story about a woman a woman like  thousands of others stranded between  fear of the past and hope for an  uncertain future  madeleine ratcliffe sky news
 

